 The primary use case for non-canonical paymasters is to enable users to pay gas with a dedicated "gas account," so that their other accounts can transact without holding any ETH. For example, a user might have a single account that holds some ETH, while other accounts only hold stablecoins and NFTs, and they can transact freely with these other accounts while using the gas account as the paymaster.
 
 Note that users can use any EOA as a paymaster thanks to the [default code](#default-code).
+
+### Guarantor mechanism
+
+Sender validation in a frame transaction may use arbitrary EVM code, which in the general case cannot be safely executed by public mempool nodes: it may read disallowed state, use banned opcodes, or have mass-invalidation dependencies. Accounts whose validation logic is not permitted by the public mempool would otherwise have to resort to private mempools.
+
+The `APPROVE_GUARANTEE` scope addresses this by letting an account commit to paying for a transaction unconditionally, even if sender validation subsequently fails. Mempool nodes simulate only the guarantor's `VERIFY` frame and do not need to understand or execute the sender's validation logic. Senders gain the freedom to implement arbitrary validation schemes while remaining eligible for public mempool propagation. Guarantors may be accounts the user owns or third parties with a trust relationship with the user.
+
+`APPROVE_GUARANTEE` is a separate scope rather than a flag bit combined with `APPROVE_PAYMENT` because the guarantor's commitment is conditional (only collected if no `APPROVE_PAYMENT` overrides it) and overridable by a later `APPROVE_PAYMENT`. A separate scope keeps override semantics unambiguous and lets the canonical paymaster select code paths by scope.
+
+#### Replay protection for guarantors
+
+When sender validation fails, the guarantor's own mechanism (not the sender's keyed nonce) must prevent replays. Otherwise the guarantor's balance could be drained by replaying a guaranteed transaction whose sender validation fails.
+
+The canonical paymaster implements this by tracking per-sender nonces via `guarantor_nonce[sender]` and requiring a `bump_nonce` frame after the sender `VERIFY` frame; the `bump_nonce` increments the mapping only when sender validation failed, avoiding state writes in the success case. The canonical paymaster also signs over the frame signature hash (`TXPARAM(0x09)`) rather than the canonical signature hash, so its signature binds the sender's `VERIFY` frame data. Without that binding, an attacker could replace the sender's `VERIFY` data with garbage to make validation fail while the guarantor still paid.
+
+#### Payer override
+
+A later `APPROVE_PAYMENT` (or `APPROVE_EXECUTION_AND_PAYMENT`) may override a previous `APPROVE_GUARANTEE`. When this happens, the protocol refunds the transaction's maximum cost to the original guarantor and collects it from the new payer.
+
+This enables a "guarantor as admission bond" pattern: a guarantor approves payment to get the transaction into the mempool, but the sender later takes over payment after performing an in-transaction action (e.g., swapping ERC-20 tokens to ETH). If the takeover fails, the `VERIFY` frame reverts, its state changes (including the payer switch) are discarded, the guarantor remains the payer, and the transaction stays valid.
+
+### Single `signer` field and `AUTH_MANAGER`
+
+Earlier drafts of EIP-8250 separate `nonce_key` and `nonce_seq`. This consolidated proposal uses a single `uint64 signer` and reuses `nonce` as the per-keyed-nonce sequence, so each keyed nonce is indexed by the same identifier used to look up registered signer pubkeys in `AUTH_MANAGER`. PQ pubkeys (lattice: 897 B-2.6 KB; multivariate: 1.2-5.5 KB) cannot directly key state, so a small `uint64 signer` ID indirects to the registered pubkey. `signer == 0` preserves the legacy ECDSA / account-nonce path byte-identically to upstream.
+
+Colocating signer entries and keyed nonces under a single `AUTH_MANAGER` reduces protocol surface from two reserved addresses, two code hashes, and two RPC roots to one. Both maps solve flavors of the same problem (resolving an account's authentication identity), so a shared system contract is a strict simplification.
 
+### Signer binding is registry-only
+
+The recovery property of secp256k1 is curve-specific. No NIST PQC scheme (ML-DSA, Falcon, SLH-DSA, MAYO) admits a recover operation. The `ECRECOVER` precompile is wired into immutable contracts (`permit`, WETH, multisigs, [ERC-1271](./eip-1271.md) fallbacks, optimistic-rollup fault proofs) that cannot be redeployed. Without a way to recognize a non-secp256k1 signer for the same address, PQ accounts are locked out of every existing `ECRECOVER` caller.
+
+Signer binding threads the needle: a non-secp256k1 `VERIFY` frame proves `(digest, address)` ahead of execution, and `ECRECOVER` looks the answer up on the hit path while preserving the miss path byte-identically. Pubkey resolution is via `AUTH_MANAGER.getSigner(target, signer)` exclusively. Inline envelope pubkeys are rejected: kilobyte-scale pubkeys multiplied per transaction would inflate mempool bandwidth and witness size, and supporting both pubkey-by-reference (lattice, multivariate) and pubkey-by-value (hash-based) would fork the protocol into two binding chains for one logical capability.
