 In general, it can be assumed that handling of frame transactions imposes similar restrictions as EIP-7702 on mempool relay, i.e. only a single transaction can be pending for an account that uses frame transactions.
 
+### Guarantor risk and griefing
+
+A guarantor backing a transaction whose sender `VERIFY` fails absorbs the gas. The canonical paymaster's guarantor-mode signature binds `TXPARAM(0x09)`, which covers the sender `VERIFY` frame's data; this prevents an attacker from mutating the sender `VERIFY` data after the guarantor signs.
+
+Guarantor commitments must be paired with a `bump_nonce` frame structurally enforced by the canonical paymaster runtime, so that the paymaster's per-sender guarantor nonce advances even when sender `VERIFY` fails. Without this, a guarantor signature would be replayable.
+
+### Keyed-nonce state
+
+Each non-zero `(sender, signer)` keyed nonce consumes one storage slot in `AUTH_MANAGER` (SSTORE-from-zero, `KEYED_NONCE_FIRST_USE_GAS = 20000`). State growth is bounded economically by this surcharge and by the public mempool's per-`(sender, signer, nonce)` triple admission policy. `tx.nonce = MAX_NONCE - 1` (= `2^64 - 2`) is the last valid sequence on a keyed nonce; senders migrate to a new `signer` value before reaching it. Exhaustion is unreachable in practice.
+
+### Signer binding scope
+
+The verified-signers table is transaction-scoped and rebuilt per transaction. There is no persistent recognition of `(digest, address)` bindings across transactions; each transaction must include the binding `VERIFY` frame that proves the claim. `MAX_BOUND_SIGNERS = 8` bounds table-population cost.
